	date = {2014/05/01},
	date-added = {2015-01-22 04:35:05 +0000},
	date-modified = {2015-01-22 04:35:05 +0000},
	journal = {Geological Society of America Bulletin},
	m3 = {10.1130/B30949.1},
	month = {05},
	number = {5-6},
	pages = {813--830},
	title = {O2 constraints from Paleoproterozoic detrital pyrite and uraninite},
	ty = {JOUR},
	url = {http://gsabulletin.gsapubs.org/content/126/5-6/813.abstract N2 - Redox-sensitive detrital grains such as pyrite and uraninite in sedimentary successions provide one of the most conspicuous geological clues to a different composition of the Archean and early Paleoproterozoic atmosphere. Today, these minerals are rapidly chemically weathered within short transport distances. Prior to the rise of oxygen, low O2 concentrations allowed their survival in siliciclastic deposits with grain erosion tied only to physical transport processes. After the rise of oxygen, redox-sensitive detrital grains effectively vanish from the sedimentary record. To get a better understanding of the timing of this transition, we examined sandstones recorded in a scientific drill core from the South African 2.415 Ga Koegas Subgroup, a mixed siliciclastic and iron formation–bearing unit deposited on the western deltaic margin of the Kaapvaal craton in early Paleoproterozoic time. We observed detrital pyrite and uraninite grains throughout all investigated sandstone beds in the section, indicating the rise of oxygen is younger than 2.415 Ga. To better understand how observations of detrital pyrite and uraninite in sedimentary rocks can quantitatively constrain Earth surface redox conditions, we constructed a model of grain erosion from chemical weathering and physical abrasion to place an upper limit on ancient environmental O2 concentrations. Even conservative model calculations for deltaic depositional systems with sufficient transport distances (approximately hundreds of kilometers) show that redox-sensitive detrital grains are remarkably sensitive to environmental O2 concentrations, and they constrain the Archean and early Paleoproterozoic atmosphere to have <3.2 × 10–5 atm of molecular O2. These levels are lower than previously hypothesized for redox-sensitive detrital grains, but they are consistent with estimates made from other redox proxy data, including the anomalous fractionation of sulfur isotopes. The binary loss of detrital pyrite and uraninite from the sedimentary record coincident with the rise of oxygen indicates that atmospheric O2 concentrations rose substantially at this time and were never again sufficiently low (<0.01 atm) to enable survival and preservation of these grains in short transport systems.},
	volume = {126},
	year = {2014},
	bdsk-file-1 = {},
	bdsk-url-1 = {http://gsabulletin.gsapubs.org/content/126/5-6/813.abstract%20N2%20-%20Redox-sensitive%20detrital%20grains%20such%20as%20pyrite%20and%20uraninite%20in%20sedimentary%20successions%20provide%20one%20of%20the%20most%20conspicuous%20geological%20clues%20to%20a%20different%20composition%20of%20the%20Archean%20and%20early%20Paleoproterozoic%20atmosphere.%20Today,%20these%20minerals%20are%20rapidly%20chemically%20weathered%20within%20short%20transport%20distances.%20Prior%20to%20the%20rise%20of%20oxygen,%20low%20O2%20concentrations%20allowed%20their%20survival%20in%20siliciclastic%20deposits%20with%20grain%20erosion%20tied%20only%20to%20physical%20transport%20processes.%20After%20the%20rise%20of%20oxygen,%20redox-sensitive%20detrital%20grains%20effectively%20vanish%20from%20the%20sedimentary%20record.%20To%20get%20a%20better%20understanding%20of%20the%20timing%20of%20this%20transition,%20we%20examined%20sandstones%20recorded%20in%20a%20scientific%20drill%20core%20from%20the%20South%20African%202.415%20Ga%20Koegas%20Subgroup,%20a%20mixed%20siliciclastic%20and%20iron%20formation%E2%80%93bearing%20unit%20deposited%20on%20the%20western%20deltaic%20margin%20of%20the%20Kaapvaal%20craton%20in%20early%20Paleoproterozoic%20time.%20We%20observed%20detrital%20pyrite%20and%20uraninite%20grains%20throughout%20all%20investigated%20sandstone%20beds%20in%20the%20section,%20indicating%20the%20rise%20of%20oxygen%20is%20younger%20than%202.415%20Ga.%20To%20better%20understand%20how%20observations%20of%20detrital%20pyrite%20and%20uraninite%20in%20sedimentary%20rocks%20can%20quantitatively%20constrain%20Earth%20surface%20redox%20conditions,%20we%20constructed%20a%20model%20of%20grain%20erosion%20from%20chemical%20weathering%20and%20physical%20abrasion%20to%20place%20an%20upper%20limit%20on%20ancient%20environmental%20O2%20concentrations.%20Even%20conservative%20model%20calculations%20for%20deltaic%20depositional%20systems%20with%20sufficient%20transport%20distances%20(approximately%20hundreds%20of%20kilometers)%20show%20that%20redox-sensitive%20detrital%20grains%20are%20remarkably%20sensitive%20to%20environmental%20O2%20concentrations,%20and%20they%20constrain%20the%20Archean%20and%20early%20Paleoproterozoic%20atmosphere%20to%20have%20%3C3.2%20%C3%97%2010%E2%80%935%20atm%20of%20molecular%20O2.%20These%20levels%20are%20lower%20than%20previously%20hypothesized%20for%20redox-sensitive%20detrital%20grains,%20but%20they%20are%20consistent%20with%20estimates%20made%20from%20other%20redox%20proxy%20data,%20including%20the%20anomalous%20fractionation%20of%20sulfur%20isotopes.%20The%20binary%20loss%20of%20detrital%20pyrite%20and%20uraninite%20from%20the%20sedimentary%20record%20coincident%20with%20the%20rise%20of%20oxygen%20indicates%20that%20atmospheric%20O2%20concentrations%20rose%20substantially%20at%20this%20time%20and%20were%20never%20again%20sufficiently%20low%20(%3C0.01%20atm)%20to%20enable%20survival%20and%20preservation%20of%20these%20grains%20in%20short%20transport%20systems.}}

@article{Holbourn2015a,
	author = {Holbourn, Ann and Kuhnt, Wolfgang and Kochhann, Karlos G. D. and Andersen, Nils and Sebastian Meier, K. J.},
	date = {2015/02/01},
	date-added = {2015-01-20 19:43:06 +0000},
	date-modified = {2015-01-20 19:43:06 +0000},
	journal = {Geology},
	m3 = {10.1130/G36317.1},
	month = {02},
	number = {2},
	pages = {123--126},
	title = {Global perturbation of the carbon cycle at the onset of the Miocene Climatic Optimum},
	ty = {JOUR},
	url = {http://geology.gsapubs.org/content/43/2/123.abstract N2 - The Miocene Climatic Optimum (MCO; ca. 17–14.7 Ma) represents one of several major interruptions in the long-term cooling trend of the past 50 m.y. To date, the processes driving high-amplitude climate variability and sustaining global warmth during this remarkable interval remain highly enigmatic. We present high-resolution benthic foraminiferal and bulk carbonate stable isotope records in an exceptional, continuous, carbonate-rich sedimentary archive (Integrated Ocean Drilling Program Site U1337, eastern equatorial Pacific Ocean), which offer a new view of climate evolution over the onset of the MCO. A sharp decline in δ18O and δ13C at ca. 16.9 Ma, contemporaneous with a massive increase in carbonate dissolution, demonstrates that abrupt warming was coupled to an intense perturbation of the carbon cycle. The rapid recovery in δ13C at ca. 16.7 Ma, ∼250 k.y. after the beginning of the MCO, marks the onset of the first carbon isotope maximum within the long-lasting “Monterey Excursion.” These results lend support to the notion that atmospheric pCO2 variations drove profound changes in the global carbon reservoir through the MCO, implying a delicate balance between changing CO2 fluxes, rates of silicate weathering, and global carbon sequestration. Comparison with a high-resolution δ13C record spanning the onset of the Cretaceous Oceanic Anoxic Event 1a (∼120 m.y. ago) reveals common forcing factors and climatic responses, providing a long-term perspective to understand climate–carbon cycle feedbacks during warmer periods of Earth’s climate with markedly different atmospheric CO2 concentrations.},
	volume = {43},
	year = {2015},
	bdsk-file-1 = {},
	bdsk-url-1 = {http://geology.gsapubs.org/content/43/2/123.abstract%20N2%20-%20The%20Miocene%20Climatic%20Optimum%20(MCO;%20ca.%2017%E2%80%9314.7%20Ma)%20represents%20one%20of%20several%20major%20interruptions%20in%20the%20long-term%20cooling%20trend%20of%20the%20past%2050%20m.y.%20To%20date,%20the%20processes%20driving%20high-amplitude%20climate%20variability%20and%20sustaining%20global%20warmth%20during%20this%20remarkable%20interval%20remain%20highly%20enigmatic.%20We%20present%20high-resolution%20benthic%20foraminiferal%20and%20bulk%20carbonate%20stable%20isotope%20records%20in%20an%20exceptional,%20continuous,%20carbonate-rich%20sedimentary%20archive%20(Integrated%20Ocean%20Drilling%20Program%20Site%20U1337,%20eastern%20equatorial%20Pacific%20Ocean),%20which%20offer%20a%20new%20view%20of%20climate%20evolution%20over%20the%20onset%20of%20the%20MCO.%20A%20sharp%20decline%20in%20%CE%B418O%20and%20%CE%B413C%20at%20ca.%2016.9%20Ma,%20contemporaneous%20with%20a%20massive%20increase%20in%20carbonate%20dissolution,%20demonstrates%20that%20abrupt%20warming%20was%20coupled%20to%20an%20intense%20perturbation%20of%20the%20carbon%20cycle.%20The%20rapid%20recovery%20in%20%CE%B413C%20at%20ca.%2016.7%20Ma,%20%E2%88%BC250%20k.y.%20after%20the%20beginning%20of%20the%20MCO,%20marks%20the%20onset%20of%20the%20first%20carbon%20isotope%20maximum%20within%20the%20long-lasting%20%E2%80%9CMonterey%20Excursion.%E2%80%9D%20These%20results%20lend%20support%20to%20the%20notion%20that%20atmospheric%20pCO2%20variations%20drove%20profound%20changes%20in%20the%20global%20carbon%20reservoir%20through%20the%20MCO,%20implying%20a%20delicate%20balance%20between%20changing%20CO2%20fluxes,%20rates%20of%20silicate%20weathering,%20and%20global%20carbon%20sequestration.%20Comparison%20with%20a%20high-resolution%20%CE%B413C%20record%20spanning%20the%20onset%20of%20the%20Cretaceous%20Oceanic%20Anoxic%20Event%201a%20(%E2%88%BC120%20m.y.%20ago)%20reveals%20common%20forcing%20factors%20and%20climatic%20responses,%20providing%20a%20long-term%20perspective%20to%20understand%20climate%E2%80%93carbon%20cycle%20feedbacks%20during%20warmer%20periods%20of%20Earth%E2%80%99s%20climate%20with%20markedly%20different%20atmospheric%20CO2%20concentrations.}}

@article{Fu2014a,
	annote = {10.1126/science.1258022},
	author = {Fu, Roger R. and Weiss, Benjamin P. and Lima, Eduardo A. and Harrison, Richard J. and Bai, Xue-Ning and Desch, Steven J. and Ebel, Denton S. and Suavet, Cl{\'e}ment and Wang, Huapei and Glenn, David and Le Sage, David and Kasama, Takeshi and Walsworth, Ronald L. and Kuan, Aaron T.},
	date = {2014/11/28},
	date-added = {2015-01-15 21:25:55 +0000},
	date-modified = {2015-01-15 21:25:55 +0000},
	journal = {Science},
	journal1 = {Science},
	month = {11},
	number = {6213},
	pages = {1089--1092},
	title = {Solar nebula magnetic fields recorded in the Semarkona meteorite},
	ty = {JOUR},
	url = {http://www.sciencemag.org/content/346/6213/1089.abstract N2 - Magnetic fields are proposed to have played a critical role in some of the most enigmatic processes of planetary formation by mediating the rapid accretion of disk material onto the central star and the formation of the first solids. However, there have been no experimental constraints on the intensity of these fields. Here we show that dusty olivine-bearing chondrules from the Semarkona meteorite were magnetized in a nebular field of 54 ± 21 microteslas. This intensity supports chondrule formation by nebular shocks or planetesimal collisions rather than by electric currents, the x-wind, or other mechanisms near the Sun. This implies that background magnetic fields in the terrestrial planet-forming region were likely 5 to 54 microteslas, which is sufficient to account for measured rates of mass and angular momentum transport in protoplanetary disks.},
	volume = {346},
	year = {2014},
	bdsk-file-1 = {},
	bdsk-file-2 = {},
	bdsk-url-1 = {http://www.sciencemag.org/content/346/6213/1089.abstract%20N2%20-%20Magnetic%20fields%20are%20proposed%20to%20have%20played%20a%20critical%20role%20in%20some%20of%20the%20most%20enigmatic%20processes%20of%20planetary%20formation%20by%20mediating%20the%20rapid%20accretion%20of%20disk%20material%20onto%20the%20central%20star%20and%20the%20formation%20of%20the%20first%20solids.%20However,%20there%20have%20been%20no%20experimental%20constraints%20on%20the%20intensity%20of%20these%20fields.%20Here%20we%20show%20that%20dusty%20olivine-bearing%20chondrules%20from%20the%20Semarkona%20meteorite%20were%20magnetized%20in%20a%20nebular%20field%20of%2054%20%C2%B1%2021%20microteslas.%20This%20intensity%20supports%20chondrule%20formation%20by%20nebular%20shocks%20or%20planetesimal%20collisions%20rather%20than%20by%20electric%20currents,%20the%20x-wind,%20or%20other%20mechanisms%20near%20the%20Sun.%20This%20implies%20that%20background%20magnetic%20fields%20in%20the%20terrestrial%20planet-forming%20region%20were%20likely%205%20to%2054%20microteslas,%20which%20is%20sufficient%20to%20account%20for%20measured%20rates%20of%20mass%20and%20angular%20momentum%20transport%20in%20protoplanetary%20disks.}}

@article{Luo2015a,
	abstract = {Abstract Multiple-sulfur isotope compositions (32S, 33S, 34S and 36S) were analyzed for paired carbonate-associated sulfate (CAS) and disseminated pyrite (PY) from the ∼1.6-Ga Gaoyuzhuang Formation of the North China Craton to reconstruct the history of sulfate levels in Proterozoic oceans. The 200-m-thick study interval yielded relatively constant values for δ34SCAS (13.0 $\pm$ 1.8‰), δ34SPY (8.0 $\pm$ 2.3‰), and Δ34SCAS-PY (∼5‰), as well as relatively constant Δ33S (0 $\pm$ 0.05‰) and Δ36S (0.35 $\pm$ 0.15‰) for both CAS and pyrite. Limited variation in δ34SPY and slightly lower Δ33S of pyrite relative to CAS suggest water-column precipitation of pyrite. Limited fractionation of sulfur during microbial sulfate reduction (as documented by Δ34SCAS-PY) implies low seawater sulfate concentrations in the early Mesoproterozoic ocean. We quantitatively constrained paleo-seawater [SO42−] using a novel modeling approach based on measured values of Δ34SCAS-PY and ∂δ34SCAS/∂t(max). For the study unit, Δ34SCAS-PY is 5.4 $\pm$ 1.4‰ (n = 17), and ∂δ34SCAS/∂t(max) is 6.8--34‰ Myr−1 based on sedimentation rates of 30--150 m Myr−1. These data indicate early Mesoproterozoic seawater [SO42−] of ∼&lt;0.1 to 0.35 mM (with a maximum possible concentration of 1.8 mM), a range that is lower and more tightly constrained than earlier estimates for the Mesoproterozoic. Compilation of published data suggests that low seawater sulfate concentrations began about ∼1.7 Ga and persisted until at least the mid-Mesoproterozoic (∼1.4 Ga), documenting a distinct early Mesoproterozoic perturbation in ocean chemistry that may have been related to a decline in atmospheric pO2 after Great Oxidation Event I.},
	author = {Luo, Genming and Ono, Shuhei and Huang, Junhua and Algeo, Thomas J. and Li, Chao and Zhou, Lian and Robinson, Andrew and Lyons, Timothy W. and Xie, Shucheng},
	date = {2015/3//},
	date-added = {2015-01-13 20:33:08 +0000},
	date-modified = {2015-01-13 20:33:08 +0000},
	doi = {http://dx.doi.org/10.1016/j.precamres.2014.12.014},
	isbn = {0301-9268},
	journal = {Precambrian Research},
	keywords = {Seawater sulfate; Multiple sulfur isotopes; Proterozoic; Carbonate associated sulfate; North China Craton},
	month = {3},
	number = {0},
	pages = {36--47},
	title = {Decline in oceanic sulfate levels during the early Mesoproterozoic},
	ty = {JOUR},
	url = {http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0301926814004677},
	volume = {258},
	year = {2015},
	bdsk-file-1 = {},
	bdsk-url-1 = {http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0301926814004677},
	bdsk-url-2 = {http://dx.doi.org/10.1016/j.precamres.2014.12.014}}

@article{George2014a,
	annote = {10.1130/G35249.1},
	author = {George, Annette D. and Chow, Nancy and Trinajstic, Kate M.},
	date = {2014/04/01},
	date-added = {2015-01-12 23:38:36 +0000},
	date-modified = {2015-01-12 23:38:36 +0000},
	journal = {Geology},
	journal1 = {Geology},
	month = {04},
	number = {4},
	pages = {327--330},
	title = {Oxic facies and the Late Devonian mass extinction, Canning Basin, Australia},
	ty = {JOUR},
	url = {http://geology.gsapubs.org/content/42/4/327.abstract N2 - The close association of anoxic or dysoxic sedimentary rocks and the major Late Devonian (Frasnian–Famennian) mass extinction has focused considerable attention on anoxia as the major cause or as a major factor in a multicausal scenario. The record of the Late Devonian biotic crisis in the well-known reef complexes of northwestern Australia (Canning Basin), in contrast to many localities elsewhere, does not display sedimentological evidence of anoxia through the Frasnian-Famennian boundary interval. Analysis of continuous drill core through this interval has yielded three positive δ13C isotopic excursions, only one of which coincides with total organic carbon (TOC) maxima in our data. Multielement geochemical proxies suggest that TOC maxima preceding positive shifts in δ13C most likely resulted from higher productivity caused by nutrient influx from continental weathering, given the close association between TOC maxima and regional relative sea-level falls. Our interpretation supports the view that anoxia was not a fundamental driver of mass extinction and stresses the importance of integrated data sets and understanding regional controls on environmental changes and/or stresses.},
	volume = {42},
	year = {2014},
	bdsk-file-1 = {},
	bdsk-url-1 = {http://geology.gsapubs.org/content/42/4/327.abstract%20N2%20-%20The%20close%20association%20of%20anoxic%20or%20dysoxic%20sedimentary%20rocks%20and%20the%20major%20Late%20Devonian%20(Frasnian%E2%80%93Famennian)%20mass%20extinction%20has%20focused%20considerable%20attention%20on%20anoxia%20as%20the%20major%20cause%20or%20as%20a%20major%20factor%20in%20a%20multicausal%20scenario.%20The%20record%20of%20the%20Late%20Devonian%20biotic%20crisis%20in%20the%20well-known%20reef%20complexes%20of%20northwestern%20Australia%20(Canning%20Basin),%20in%20contrast%20to%20many%20localities%20elsewhere,%20does%20not%20display%20sedimentological%20evidence%20of%20anoxia%20through%20the%20Frasnian-Famennian%20boundary%20interval.%20Analysis%20of%20continuous%20drill%20core%20through%20this%20interval%20has%20yielded%20three%20positive%20%CE%B413C%20isotopic%20excursions,%20only%20one%20of%20which%20coincides%20with%20total%20organic%20carbon%20(TOC)%20maxima%20in%20our%20data.%20Multielement%20geochemical%20proxies%20suggest%20that%20TOC%20maxima%20preceding%20positive%20shifts%20in%20%CE%B413C%20most%20likely%20resulted%20from%20higher%20productivity%20caused%20by%20nutrient%20influx%20from%20continental%20weathering,%20given%20the%20close%20association%20between%20TOC%20maxima%20and%20regional%20relative%20sea-level%20falls.%20Our%20interpretation%20supports%20the%20view%20that%20anoxia%20was%20not%20a%20fundamental%20driver%20of%20mass%20extinction%20and%20stresses%20the%20importance%20of%20integrated%20data%20sets%20and%20understanding%20regional%20controls%20on%20environmental%20changes%20and/or%20stresses.}}

@article{Van-Lente2009a,
	abstract = {The isolated volcano-sedimentary sequences of the Punagarh and Sindreth Groups occur along the western flank of the Delhi Fold Belt in northwest India, and include mafic rocks (pillow basalts and dolerite dykes) that are dominantly olivine tholeiites with minor quartz-normative and alkali basalts. Sindreth samples appear to have higher primary TiO2 and P2O5 abundances relative to those from Punagarh. Both suites of mafic rocks show variable, but profound hydrothermal alteration effects, with loss on ignition (LOI) values up to 10.3 wt.{\%}, and extensive secondary minerals including albite, sericite, chlorite and calcite. Despite this, there is excellent preservation of magmatic textures, but there has been extensive albitization of plagioclase phenocrysts, a hallmark of hydrothermal alteration processes in oceanic crust. Supporting evidence for such hydrothermal alteration comes from correlations of LOI abundances with CaO/Na2O, and evidence for U mobility is apparent on diagrams of Nb/Th vs. Nb/U. Felsic volcanic rocks (rhyolite, dacite) interlayered with the Sindreth basalts yield U--Pb zircon ages (TIMS method) between 761 $\pm$16 and 767 $\pm$3 Ma, which we interpret as representing the time of primary magmatic activity. We infer that the volcano-sedimentary rocks of the Punagarh also formed at this time, on the basis of similarities in lithology, stratigraphy, field relations and geochemistry. Intermediate granitoid rocks yield older U--Pb ages of 800 $\pm$2 and 873 $\pm$3 Ma, which we correlate with the post-Delhi Supergroup Erinpura Granites. Taken together, the features of the Punagarh and Sindreth Groups are consistent with their formation in a back-arc basin setting. Their coevality with other magmatic systems in NW India (Malani Igneous Suite), the Seychelles and Madagascar, for which a continental arc setting has also been proposed, supports the notion of an extensive convergent margin in western Rodinia at 750--770 Ma.},
	author = {Van Lente, B. and Ashwal, L. D. and Pandit, M. K. and Bowring, S. A. and Torsvik, T. H.},
	date = {2009/5//},
	date-added = {2015-01-12 20:51:19 +0000},
	date-modified = {2015-01-12 20:51:19 +0000},
	doi = {http://dx.doi.org/10.1016/j.precamres.2009.01.007},
	isbn = {0301-9268},
	journal = {Precambrian Research},
	keywords = {Hydrothermal alteration; Punagarh and Sindreth Groups; U--Pb geochronology; Geochemistry; Aravalli Craton; NW India},
	month = {5},
	number = {3--4},
	pages = {202--222},
	title = {Neoproterozoic hydrothermally altered basaltic rocks from Rajasthan, northwest India: Implications for late Precambrian tectonic evolution of the Aravalli Craton},
	ty = {JOUR},
	url = {http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0301926809000205},
	volume = {170},
	year = {2009},
	bdsk-file-1 = {},
	bdsk-url-1 = {http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0301926809000205},
	bdsk-url-2 = {http://dx.doi.org/10.1016/j.precamres.2009.01.007}}

@article{Meert2013a,
	abstract = {Abstract At 750 Ma India was part of a larger fragment of eastern Gondwana blocks that included the Seychelles-Mauritia, Madagascar, Sri Lanka and the Enderby Land-Prydz Bay region of East Antarctica. Subduction of the Mozambique Ocean beneath Seychelles-Mauritia, northern Madagascar and northwestern India formed a lengthy continental arc that remained active during the formation of Gondwana. Paleomagnetic data from the Malani rhyolites and associated dykes provide a robust paleomagnetic pole constraining India's position at this time. The rhyolitic and granitic rocks associated with the Malani Igneous Suite (MIS) have robust age constraints; however, the ages of the mafic dykes were inferred solely on the basis of similarity in paleomagnetic directions to the rhyolitic units. Here we present new geochronological data from the Malani mafic dykes that yield a minimum age of 704 Ma. The 207Pb/206Pb ages obtained for the dykes are less-likely to be affected by Pb-loss and yield a more reliable estimate for the age of the mafic dykes of ~ 750 Ma. We argue that intrusion of these mafic (and minor felsic) dykes represents the final pulse of MIS magmatism.  Many of the granitic rocks in the region are reported as `unclassified' due to limited geochemical data and/or geochronological ages. Some of these `unclassified' granites are intruded by the mafic dykes sampled in this study near the town of Bilara. The granites yielded zircon core ages of ~ 1100 Ma with younger rims averaging ~ 1020 Ma. We argue that this provides further evidence for a significant orogenic event ~ 1000 Ma that may relate to the collision of the Marwar block with the Banded Gneiss Complex/Bundelkhand craton in north-central India. Other ~ 1000 Ma orogenesis is also known along the Central Indian Tectonic Zone (CITZ) and the Eastern Ghats Mobile Belt. Globally, this same time interval is thought to represent the amalgamation of the supercontinent Rodinia and may also have resulted in the closure of the major ``Purana'' basins in India.},
	author = {Meert, Joseph G. and Pandit, Manoj K. and Kamenov, George D.},
	date = {2013/11/26/},
	date-added = {2015-01-12 20:49:49 +0000},
	date-modified = {2025-03-11 10:05:41 -0500},
	day = {26},
	doi = {10.1016/j.tecto.2013.06.019},
	isbn = {0040-1951},
	journal = {Tectonophysics},
	keywords = {Rodinia; Malani; Paleomagnetism; Geochronology; India},
	month = {11},
	number = {0},
	pages = {1254--1267},
	title = {{Further geochronological and paleomagnetic constraints on Malani (and pre-Malani) magmatism in NW India}},
	ty = {JOUR},
	url = {http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0040195113003843},
	volume = {608},
