	title = {Thrusted Paleozoic Rocks in the Northern and Central HD Range, Northeastern Nevada},
	ty = {JOUR},
	url = {http://gsabulletin.gsapubs.org/content/81/9/2689.abstract N2 - The northern and central HD Range in northeastern Nevada is formed of a succession of six major thrust plates that lie on Mississippian Chainman Shale in the central part and are juxtaposed or lie on Lower Permian quartzites and limestones at the northern end. Each plate has its own characteristic stratigraphy: the lowest plate consists of Ordovician chert unconformably overlain by Mississippian Diamond Peak quartzite; the succeeding three are characterized by thin, leptogeosynclinal, Ordovician-Silurian sections, or by intercalations of eu- and miogeosynclinal facies; the two upper plates have thin Ordovician-Silurian sections unconformably overlain by Pennsylvanian and Permian limestone and clastics. Three new formations have been differentiated in the Ordovician, one in the Silurian, and one in the Pennsylvanian.Directional structures and regional stratigraphic comparisons indicate that the last move of the plates, except the upper one, was in a south-southeasterly direction; more than one direction of movement is recorded within single plates, and rotation of large segments of one or more plates appears to have been common. Structures are the result of thrusting of thin, incompetent sections: imbrications, up- and overthrusting, and folding of the frontal ends of thrusts are common to many tectonic plates.The emplacement of the allochthon occurred in two stages: (a) the Ordovician and Silurian eugeosynclinal rocks were first affected in Late Devonian or Mississippian time by the Antler Orogeny and thrust eastward over the miogeosyncline from a source in southern Oregon or southwestern Idaho; subsequently, an overlap sequence of clastics and carbonates was laid down over them, (b) The allochthonous Ordovician-Silurian rocks and the overlap sequences were moved southeastward from south-central Idaho to their present position in early Mesozoic time prior to the intrusion of the mid-Jurassic Contact Pluton. This last phase of thrusting is possibly one of the earliest phases of deformation recorded in the eastern Great Basin east of the Antler Orogenic Belt and preceeded more extensive late Mesozoic thrusting in the Sevier and Laramide Belts of Utah and Wyoming. The occurrence of eugeosynclinal rocks in northeastern Nevada cannot be attributed directly to Antler thrusting: Mesozoic rethrusting of these rocks is to be considered in evaluating their tectonic history. The eastern limits of the Antler Belt do not extend to northeastern Nevada.The upper thrust plate in the north-central HD Range belongs to a separate tectonic succession in the southern part of the range and was emplaced from a more westerly source in late Mesozoic or Tertiary time.},
	volume = {81},
	year = {1970},
	bdsk-file-1 = {},
	bdsk-url-1 = {http://gsabulletin.gsapubs.org/content/81/9/2689.abstract%20N2%20-%20The%20northern%20and%20central%20HD%20Range%20in%20northeastern%20Nevada%20is%20formed%20of%20a%20succession%20of%20six%20major%20thrust%20plates%20that%20lie%20on%20Mississippian%20Chainman%20Shale%20in%20the%20central%20part%20and%20are%20juxtaposed%20or%20lie%20on%20Lower%20Permian%20quartzites%20and%20limestones%20at%20the%20northern%20end.%20Each%20plate%20has%20its%20own%20characteristic%20stratigraphy:%20the%20lowest%20plate%20consists%20of%20Ordovician%20chert%20unconformably%20overlain%20by%20Mississippian%20Diamond%20Peak%20quartzite;%20the%20succeeding%20three%20are%20characterized%20by%20thin,%20leptogeosynclinal,%20Ordovician-Silurian%20sections,%20or%20by%20intercalations%20of%20eu-%20and%20miogeosynclinal%20facies;%20the%20two%20upper%20plates%20have%20thin%20Ordovician-Silurian%20sections%20unconformably%20overlain%20by%20Pennsylvanian%20and%20Permian%20limestone%20and%20clastics.%20Three%20new%20formations%20have%20been%20differentiated%20in%20the%20Ordovician,%20one%20in%20the%20Silurian,%20and%20one%20in%20the%20Pennsylvanian.Directional%20structures%20and%20regional%20stratigraphic%20comparisons%20indicate%20that%20the%20last%20move%20of%20the%20plates,%20except%20the%20upper%20one,%20was%20in%20a%20south-southeasterly%20direction;%20more%20than%20one%20direction%20of%20movement%20is%20recorded%20within%20single%20plates,%20and%20rotation%20of%20large%20segments%20of%20one%20or%20more%20plates%20appears%20to%20have%20been%20common.%20Structures%20are%20the%20result%20of%20thrusting%20of%20thin,%20incompetent%20sections:%20imbrications,%20up-%20and%20overthrusting,%20and%20folding%20of%20the%20frontal%20ends%20of%20thrusts%20are%20common%20to%20many%20tectonic%20plates.The%20emplacement%20of%20the%20allochthon%20occurred%20in%20two%20stages:%20(a)%20the%20Ordovician%20and%20Silurian%20eugeosynclinal%20rocks%20were%20first%20affected%20in%20Late%20Devonian%20or%20Mississippian%20time%20by%20the%20Antler%20Orogeny%20and%20thrust%20eastward%20over%20the%20miogeosyncline%20from%20a%20source%20in%20southern%20Oregon%20or%20southwestern%20Idaho;%20subsequently,%20an%20overlap%20sequence%20of%20clastics%20and%20carbonates%20was%20laid%20down%20over%20them,%20(b)%20The%20allochthonous%20Ordovician-Silurian%20rocks%20and%20the%20overlap%20sequences%20were%20moved%20southeastward%20from%20south-central%20Idaho%20to%20their%20present%20position%20in%20early%20Mesozoic%20time%20prior%20to%20the%20intrusion%20of%20the%20mid-Jurassic%20Contact%20Pluton.%20This%20last%20phase%20of%20thrusting%20is%20possibly%20one%20of%20the%20earliest%20phases%20of%20deformation%20recorded%20in%20the%20eastern%20Great%20Basin%20east%20of%20the%20Antler%20Orogenic%20Belt%20and%20preceeded%20more%20extensive%20late%20Mesozoic%20thrusting%20in%20the%20Sevier%20and%20Laramide%20Belts%20of%20Utah%20and%20Wyoming.%20The%20occurrence%20of%20eugeosynclinal%20rocks%20in%20northeastern%20Nevada%20cannot%20be%20attributed%20directly%20to%20Antler%20thrusting:%20Mesozoic%20rethrusting%20of%20these%20rocks%20is%20to%20be%20considered%20in%20evaluating%20their%20tectonic%20history.%20The%20eastern%20limits%20of%20the%20Antler%20Belt%20do%20not%20extend%20to%20northeastern%20Nevada.The%20upper%20thrust%20plate%20in%20the%20north-central%20HD%20Range%20belongs%20to%20a%20separate%20tectonic%20succession%20in%20the%20southern%20part%20of%20the%20range%20and%20was%20emplaced%20from%20a%20more%20westerly%20source%20in%20late%20Mesozoic%20or%20Tertiary%20time.}}

@article{Finnegan2012b,
	abstract = {The decrease in the average magnitude of carbon isotope excursions in marine carbonates over Phanerozoic time is a longstanding unresolved problem. In addition, carbon isotope excursions commonly co-occur with oxygen isotope excursions of the same sign, implying the existence of a longstanding link between organic carbon burial fluxes and temperature. It was proposed that this connection was provided by the thermodynamic relationship between temperature and microbial respiration rates -- changes in temperature drive changes in organic carbon remineralization rate and organic carbon burial efficiency. Such a mechanism provides the logic for a positive feedback affecting the magnitude of both climate changes and carbon isotope excursions. Here, we employ feedback analysis to quantify the strength of this mechanism with modifications to a simple carbon isotope mass balance framework. We demonstrate that the potential strength of this feedback is large (perhaps several permil) for plausible ranges of historical climate change. Furthermore, our results highlight the importance of thesurface temperature boundary condition on the magnitude of the expected carbon isotope excursion. Comparisons of our model predictions with data from the terminal Eocene and Late Ordovician (Hirnantian) greenhouse--icehouse climate transitions suggestthat these excursions might be substantially explained by such a thermodynamic microbial respiration feedback. Consequently, we hypothesize that the observed pattern of decreasing excursion magnitude toward the present might be explained at least, in part, by a decrease in the mean temperature of environments of organic carbon burial driven by long-term climate and paleogeographic trends. SOMMAIRELa diminution de l'amplitude moyenne des excursions des isotopes du carbone dans les carbonates marins au fil du Phan{\'e}rozo{\"\i}que est une {\'e}nigme de longue date.  On note en outre que les excursions des isotopes du carbone coexistent couramment avec des excursions isotopiques de m{\^e}me signe de l'oxyg{\`e}ne, ce qui implique l'existence d'un lien de longue date entre les flux d'enfouissement du carbone organique et la temp{\'e}rature.  On a sugg{\'e}r{\'e} que ce lien d{\'e}coulait de la relation thermodynamique entre la temp{\'e}rature et les taux de respiration microbienne - les changements de temp{\'e}rature d{\'e}terminent le taux de remin{\'e}ralisation du carbone organique et l'efficacit{\'e} de l'enfouissement du carbone organique.  Un tel m{\'e}canisme peut expliquer la r{\'e}troaction positive affectant {\`a} la fois l'ampleur des changements climatiques et les excursions des isotopes du carbone.  Dans le cas pr{\'e}sent, nous utilisons l'analyse de la r{\'e}troaction pour quantifier la robustesse de ce m{\'e}canisme avec des modifications d'un simple bilan de masse des isotopes du carbone.  Nous d{\'e}montrons que la robustesse potentielle de cette r{\'e}troaction est forte (peut-{\^e}tre plusieurs pour mille) dans les gammes plausibles du changement climatique historique.  De plus, nos r{\'e}sultats mettent en {\'e}vidence l'importance de la condition aux limites de la temp{\'e}rature de surface sur l'ampleur de l'excursion isotopique du carbone attendue.  Les comparaisons des pr{\'e}dictions de notre mod{\`e}le avec les donn{\'e}es de la fin de l'{\'E}oc{\`e}ne et de la fin de l'Ordovicien (Hirnantien) des transitions climatiques {\`a} effet de serre-effet/de glaciation permettent de penser que ces excursions pourraient {\^e}tre correctement expliqu{\'e}es par une telle r{\'e}troaction de la thermodynamique de la respiration microbienne.  Par cons{\'e}quent, nous {\'e}mettons l'hypoth{\`e}se que la tendance observ{\'e}e de diminution de l'ampleur de l'amplitude des excursions du pass{\'e} vers le pr{\'e}sent peut s'expliquer, au moins en partie, par une diminution de la temp{\'e}rature moyenne du milieu d'enfouissement du carbone organique engendr{\'e}e par des tendances climatiques et pal{\'e}og{\'e}ographiques {\`a} long terme.},
	author = {Seth Finnegan and David Fike and David Jones and Woodward Fischer},
	date-added = {2012-12-10 20:46:37 +0000},
	date-modified = {2012-12-10 20:46:40 +0000},
	issn = {1911-4850},
	journal = {Geoscience Canada},
	number = {3},
	title = {A Temperature-Dependent Positive Feedback on the Magnitude of Carbon Isotope Excursions},
	url = {http://journals.hil.unb.ca/index.php/GC/article/view/19395},
	volume = {39},
	year = {2012},
	bdsk-file-1 = {},
	bdsk-url-1 = {http://journals.hil.unb.ca/index.php/GC/article/view/19395}}

