	bdsk-file-1 = {},
	bdsk-url-1 = {http://www.pnas.org/content/99/22/14020.abstract%20N2%20-%20If%20relationships%20exist%20between%20the%20frequency%20of%20geomagnetic%20reversals%20and%20the%20morphology,%20secular%20variation,%20and%20intensity%20of%20Earth's%20magnetic%20field,%20they%20should%20be%20best%20expressed%20during%20superchrons,%20intervals%20tens%20of%20millions%20of%20years%20long%20lacking%20reversals.%20Here%20we%20report%20paleomagnetic%20and%20paleointensity%20data%20from%20lavas%20of%20the%20Cretaceous%20Normal%20Polarity%20Superchron%20that%20formed%20at%20high%20latitudes%20near%20the%20tangent%20cylinder%20that%20surrounds%20the%20solid%20inner%20core.%20The%20time-averaged%20field%20recorded%20by%20these%20lavas%20is%20remarkably%20strong%20and%20stable.%20When%20combined%20with%20global%20results%20available%20from%20lower%20latitudes,%20these%20data%20define%20a%20time-averaged%20field%20that%20is%20overwhelmingly%20dominated%20by%20the%20axial%20dipole%20(octupole%20components%20are%20insignificant).%20These%20observations%20suggest%20that%20the%20basic%20features%20of%20the%20geomagnetic%20field%20are%20intrinsically%20related.%20Superchrons%20may%20reflect%20times%20when%20the%20nature%20of%20core%E2%80%93mantle%20boundary%20heat%20flux%20allows%20the%20geodynamo%20to%20operate%20at%20peak%20efficiency.}}

