	bdsk-url-1 = {http://geology.gsapubs.org/content/36/1/47.abstract%20N2%20-%20The%20snowball%20Earth%20theory,%20initially%20proposed%20by%20J.L.%20Kirschvink%20to%20explain%20the%20Neoproterozoic%20glacial%20episodes,%20suggests%20that%20the%20Earth%20was%20globally%20ice%20covered%20at%20720%20Ma%20(Sturtian%20episode)%20and%20640%20Ma%20(Marinoan%20episode).%20The%20reduction%20of%20the%20water%20cycle%20and%20the%20growth%20of%20large%20ice%20sheets%20led%20to%20a%20collapse%20of%20CO2%20consumption%20through%20continental%20weathering%20and%20biological%20carbon%20pumping.%20As%20a%20consequence,%20atmospheric%20CO2%20built%20up%20linearly%20to%20levels%20allowing%20escape%20from%20a%20snowball%20Earth.%20In%20this%20contribution,%20we%20question%20this%20assumed%20linear%20accumulation%20of%20CO2%20into%20the%20atmosphere.%20Using%20a%20numerical%20model%20of%20the%20carbon-alkalinity%20cycles,%20we%20suggest%20that%20during%20global%20glaciations,%20even%20a%20limited%20area%20of%20open%20waters%20(103%20km2)%20allows%20an%20efficient%20atmospheric%20CO2%20diffusion%20into%20the%20ocean.%20This%20exchange%20implies%20that%20the%20CO2%20consumption%20through%20the%20low-temperature%20alteration%20of%20the%20oceanic%20crust%20persists%20throughout%20the%20glaciation.%20Furthermore,%20our%20model%20shows%20that%20rising%20CO2%20during%20the%20glaciation%20increases%20the%20efficiency%20of%20this%20sink%20through%20the%20seawater%20acidification.%20As%20a%20result,%20the%20atmospheric%20CO2%20evolution%20is%20asymptotic,%20limiting%20the%20growth%20rate%20of%20the%20atmospheric%20carbon%20reservoir.%20Even%20after%20the%20maximum%20estimated%20duration%20of%20the%20glaciation%20(30%20m.y.),%20the%20atmospheric%20CO2%20is%20far%20from%20reaching%20the%20minimum%20deglaciation%20threshold%20(0.29%20bar).%20Accounting%20for%20this%20previously%20neglected%20carbon%20sink,%20processes%20that%20decrease%20the%20CO2%20deglaciation%20threshold%20must%20be%20further%20explored.}}

