	bdsk-url-1 = {http://www.sciencemag.org/content/335/6064/73.abstract%20N2%20-%20The%20continental%20lithosphere%20contains%20the%20oldest%20and%20most%20stable%20structures%20on%20Earth,%20where%20fragments%20of%20ancient%20material%20have%20eluded%20destruction%20by%20tectonic%20and%20surface%20processes%20operating%20over%20billions%20of%20years.%20Although%20present-day%20erosion%20of%20these%20remnants%20is%20slow,%20a%20record%20of%20how%20they%20have%20uplifted,%20eroded,%20and%20cooled%20over%20Earth%E2%80%99s%20history%20can%20provide%20insight%20into%20the%20physical%20properties%20of%20the%20continents%20and%20the%20forces%20operating%20to%20exhume%20them%20over%20geologic%20time.%20We%20constructed%20a%20continuous%20record%20of%20ancient%20lithosphere%20cooling%20with%20the%20use%20of%20uranium-lead%20(U-Pb)%20thermochronology%20on%20volcanically%20exhumed%20lower%20crustal%20fragments.%20Combining%20these%20measurements%20with%20thermal%20and%20Pb-diffusion%20models%20constrains%20the%20range%20of%20possible%20erosion%20histories.%20Measured%20U-Pb%20data%20are%20consistent%20with%20extremely%20low%20erosion%20rates%20persisting%20over%20time%20scales%20approaching%20the%20age%20of%20the%20continents%20themselves.}}

