	bdsk-url-1 = {http://sp.lyellcollection.org/content/206/1/77.abstract%20N2%20-%20Recent%20plate%20tectonic%20models%20advocate%20assembly%20of%20Proterozoic%20Australia%20by%20tectonic%20processes%20that%20involved%20large-scale%20horizontal%20motions,%20whereas%20previous%20models%20suggested%20that%20the%20continent%20evolved%20as%20an%20essentially%20intact%20block%20of%20lithosphere.%20Geological%20and%20geochemical%20observations%20alone%20are%20insufficient%20to%20test%20whether%20the%20major%20cratonic%20blocks%20of%20Australia%20were%20together%20or%20widely%20separated%20during%20the%20Proterozoic;%20only%20palaeomagnetism%20can%20provide%20quantitative%20constraints%20on%20relative%20plate%20motions%20during%20the%20Precambrian.%20Despite%20deficiencies%20in%20the%20palaeomagnetic%20record%20for%20Proterozoic%20Australia,%20groups%20of%20overlapping%20palaeopoles%20for%201.7%E2%80%931.8%20and%201.5%E2%80%931.6%20Ga%20permit%20the%20North%20and%20West%20Australian%20cratonic%20assemblages%20to%20have%20occupied%20their%20present%20relative%20positions%20since%20at%20least%20c.%201.7Ga,%20and%20to%20have%20been%20joined%20to%20the%20South%20Australian%20cratonic%20assemblage%20since%20at%20least%20c.%201.5Ga.%20Nonetheless,%20additional%20geological,%20geochronological%20and%20palaeomagnetic%20data%20are%20required%20to%20test%20whether%20large%20oceans%20closed%20between%20any%20of%20the%20continental%20blocks.}}

