	bdsk-file-1 = {},
	bdsk-file-2 = {},
	bdsk-url-1 = {http://geology.gsapubs.org/content/39/1/75.abstract%20N2%20-%20The%20primary%20succession%20history%20of%20magnetotactic%20bacteria%20(MTB)%20is%20reconstructed%20in%20postglacial%20lake%20sediments%20using%20a%20rock%20magnetic%20approach%20that%20discriminates%20biologically%20produced%20magnetite%20from%20nonorganic%20magnetic%20carriers.%20MTB%20are%20among%20the%20oldest%20prokaryotes%20found%20in%20the%20fossil%20record,%20but%20little%20is%20known%20about%20how%20they%20have%20colonized%20and%20recolonized%20habitats%20around%20the%20world.%20Here%20we%20observe%20how%20MTB%20synchronously%20colonized%204%20freshwater%20lakes%209760%20%C2%B1%20160%20yr%20ago.%20The%20lakes%20are%20more%20than%201400%20km%20apart,%20representing%20both%20coastal%20and%20inland%20regions,%20and%20have%20altitudinal%20differences%20of%20almost%20800%20m.%20The%20synchronous%20colonization%20of%20Norway%20(and%20possibly%20Sweden)%20suggests%20that%20the%20pathways%20were%20extremely%20efficient,%20and%20that%20the%20sources%20must%20have%20been%20wide%20ranging.%20We%20propose%20that%20birds%20could%20have%20carried%20and%20spread%20the%20bacteria%20as%20northward%20migration%20routes%20were%20reestablished%20following%20the%20onset%20of%20the%20current%20interglacial.%20This%20unique%20data%20set%20underscores%20the%20tenacity%20of%20MTB%20as%20evolutionary%20survivors,%20and%20also%20demonstrates%20their%20response%20to%20large-scale%20environmental%20changes%20in%20ways%20not%20previously%20anticipated.}}

