	bdsk-file-1 = {},
	bdsk-url-1 = {http://geology.gsapubs.org/content/37/7/595.abstract%20N2%20-%20Repeated%20amalgamation%20and%20subsequent%20breakup%20of%20continental%20lithosphere%20have%20profoundly%20affected%20Earth's%20evolution%20since%20the%20Archean.%20Following%20breakup,%20distinctive%20rift%20and%20passive%20margin%20sequences%20along%20the%20trailing%20edges%20of%20dispersing%20continents%20have%20been%20used%20to%20identify%20such%20margins%20in%20the%20geologic%20past.%20Using%20western%20North%20America%20as%20an%20analogue,%20we%20show%20that%20the%20leading%20edges%20of%20dispersing%20continents%20have%20isotopic%20characteristics%20that%20can%20likewise%20be%20used%20to%20identify%20these%20margins.%20For%20example,%20the%20Sm-Nd%20isotopic%20signatures%20of%20Late%20Neoproterozoic%20and%20early%20Paleozoic%20igneous%20rocks%20along%20the%20northern%20margin%20of%20Gondwana%20indicate%20derivation%20from%200.7%20to%201.1%20Ga%20old%20mantle%20lithosphere.%20This%20lithosphere%20originated%20in%20the%20Mirovoi%20Ocean%20surrounding%20Rodinia.%20It%20subsequently%20accreted%20to%20northern%20Gondwana%20in%20response%20to%20Rodinia%20breakup,%20and%20provided%20a%20source%20for%20subsequent%20magmatism.%20Accretion%20and%20subsequent%20recycling%20of%20oceanic%20mantle%20lithosphere%20should%20be%20common%20along%20the%20leading%20edges%20of%20dispersing%20continents%20following%20supercontinent%20breakup,%20providing%20an%20additional%20aid%20in%20paleocontinental%20reconstructions.}}

