@article{Tomazelli2007a,
	annote = {"Along most of the Brazilian coastline, this event [the 5e highstand] is mainly represented by marine terraces showing small variations in elevation above present sea level: 6 to 8 m (Bittencourt et al.,
1979); 7 $\pm$ 1 m (Martin et al., 1982); 8 $\pm$ 2 m (Suguio et al., 2005). On the Rio Grande do Sul (RS) Coastal Plain, in the south of Brazil, this global event is recorded as a prominent morphological feature --- a sandy coastal barrier that can be traced for hundreds of kilometers along the coast (Fig. 1). "

"The deposits can be recognized and mapped practically along all the coastal plain due to their distinctive geomorphic and sedimentologic characteristics, and stratigraphic position. These properties complemented by some thermoluminescence dating (Poupeau et al., 1988; Buchmann and Tomazelli, 2003) indicate deposition during the last interglacial event."

"Considering that the highest exposure of Ophimorpha burrows is a good indicator of the average low
tidal level, and assuming that during the Pleistocene the tidal range was the same as today (0.50 m), the last interglacial sea level along the RS coast was slightly higher than 7.7 m above present sea level. " Table 1 shows outcrop positions and maximum Ophimorpha elevation.

"The basal eolian coastal dune deposits immediately covering the fore-shore sands at one of the pit sites was dated by Thermoluminescence (TL) as 85,075 yr, with a 30% precision according to Poupeau et al. (1988). A foreshore deposit of the Last Interglacial Barrier in RS was dated by TL as 109,000 $\pm$ 7500 yr near Concei{\c c}{\~a}o Lighthouse (see Fig. 1 for location) distant 250 km to the south of the study area (Buchmann and Tomazelli, 2003). Considering that this barrier is almost continuous in the 620 km long coast of RS, and that it occurs immediately at the back of the Holocene lagoon-barrier system we strongly suggest that the studied sedimentary sequences were formed during the last interglacial (Oxygen Isotope Stage 5e) at approximately 125 ka."
},
